{"name":"Aegean Sea","hex":"#508fa2","fetched":"2026-10-19","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Aegean Sea","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A sea in the northeastern part of the Mediterranean."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Aegean_Sea"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aegean Sea","blocks":[{"text":"The Aegean Sea is an elongated embayment of the Mediterranean Sea between Europe and Asia. It is located between the Balkans and Anatolia, and covers an area of some 215,000 km2 (83,000 sq mi). In the north, the Aegean is connected to the Marmara Sea, which in turn connects to the Black Sea, by the straits of the Dardanelles and the Bosphorus, respectively. The Aegean Islands are located within the sea and some bound it on its southern periphery, including Crete and Rhodes. The sea reaches a maximum depth of 2,639 m (8,658 ft) to the west of Karpathos. The Thracian Sea and the Sea of Crete are main subdivisions of the Aegean Sea."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Location_of_the_Aegean_Sea.png/330px-Location_of_the_Aegean_Sea.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aegean_Sea","wikidata":"Q34575"},{"title":"Aegean Sea (theme)","blocks":[{"text":"The Theme of the Aegean Sea was a Byzantine province in the northern Aegean Sea, established in the mid-9th century. As one of the Byzantine Empire's three dedicated naval themes, it served chiefly to provide ships and troops for the Byzantine navy, but also served as a civil administrative circumscription."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Byzantine_Greece_ca_900_AD.svg/330px-Byzantine_Greece_ca_900_AD.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aegean_Sea_(theme)","wikidata":"Q254304"},{"title":"Sea","blocks":[{"text":"A sea is a very large body of salty water. The term has two distinct meanings. The first is the interconnected body of seawater that spans most of Earth, known as the sea or the ocean. The second is a named body of water: usually either a marginal sea, or a large, nearly landlocked body of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg/330px-Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea","wikidata":"Q165"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Karpathos_Mountains_MO.jpg/500px-Karpathos_Mountains_MO.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Karpathos_Mountains_MO.jpg/1280px-Karpathos_Mountains_MO.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Karpathos_Mountains_MO.jpg/500px-Karpathos_Mountains_MO.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Karpathos mountains meets the Aegean sea","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKarpathos_Mountains_MO.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Acropolis_wide_view.jpg/500px-Acropolis_wide_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Acropolis_wide_view.jpg/1280px-Acropolis_wide_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Acropolis_wide_view.jpg/500px-Acropolis_wide_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Athens","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAcropolis_wide_view.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Skyscrapers_in_Izmir_-_Turkey.jpg/500px-Sk